steps.body:
You rewrote the loop that converts a day's prices, and you want to know whether it got faster. One run proves little. Time it with `<chrono>`:

```cpp
std::vector<double> prices(1'000'000, 101.25);
auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
double total = 0;
for (double p : prices) {
    total += p;
}
auto stop = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(stop - start).count();
std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2) << total << '\n';  // 101250000.00, while ms changes every run
```

- `std::chrono::steady_clock` only ever moves forward. `system_clock` follows the wall clock, which the operating system may step forward or back to correct it, so never time code with it.
- `std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>` turns the difference into milliseconds as a `double`. `std::micro` and `std::nano` give microseconds and nanoseconds.

Three traps wait for every benchmark:

- **Warm-up.** The first run pays one-time costs: the operating system mapping fresh memory into the program page by page, caches that start cold. Run the work once before timing, or time several runs and look past the first.
- **Noise.** Other programs, the CPU changing its clock speed, and interrupts make each run differ. Repeat, and keep the best run, the one with the least interference. Look at the median too when the spread matters.
- **The wrong build.** A time without optimization means little. This sandbox builds a file at `-O0` unless its first line is exactly `// -O2` (or `-O1`, `-O3`, `-Os`), which builds it the way `g++ -O2` or `clang++ -O2` does in a terminal.

A checked output can't hold a time, so the examples here print a checksum and leave the line that prints the time commented out: uncomment it and run the example to see yours. On a Mac this sandbox runs its x86-64 code emulated, so its times are rough, and your own terminal's will differ.

